tier: strong
work_class: money-path
branch: feat/free-tier-quota-spill
depends_on: COST-RANK-AUTO
consumes: METER-MODEL-PROVIDER — the quota/spend-remaining BALANCE signal this ticket tracks per
  provider SOURCES from METER-MODEL-PROVIDER's per-provider metered spend (paid $) alongside quota.py's
  rate/quota-remaining (free). The meter is the sensor; this ticket is the demotion/spill actuator that
  reads it. (No owns overlap — annotation only.)
real-dep: COST-RANK-AUTO — its cost_class enum (free-daily / expiring / prepaid / metered) is the
  classification axis this ticket tracks a remaining-balance against; spill policy extends that enum's
  ordering. Build the quota tracker on top of cost_class, not a parallel scheme.
owns: src/charon/quota.py, src/charon/gateway.py, tests/test_quota_spill.py
accept: PYTHONPATH=src python3 -m pytest tests/test_quota_spill.py -v -q
prompt: /home/stack/charon-private/prompts/free-tier-quota-spill.md
scope: The mechanism behind [use-free-tiers-to-their-limits] + the Cline→Featherless layering:
  track each free/cheap-tier backend's REMAINING quota (daily/weekly/monthly and per-minute/day rate
  caps, e.g. cerebras 5 req/min, groq 14.4k req/day) like a BALANCE — SOURCING the spend/quota-remaining
  signal from METER-MODEL-PROVIDER (per-provider metered spend) + quota.py (rate/quota) — consume free
  before paid, and
  AUTO-SPILL to the next capable backend when a free tier hits its wall instead of erroring. Generalize
  the P3 balance-monitoring work from "balance (paid $)" to "resource availability" = balance (paid) +
  quota/rate-remaining (free), so the gateway sort/failover treats an exhausted free tier as unavailable
  (skip + spill) rather than a hard error. Capability-matched only: a free rank-0 member must NOT front a
  closed premium pool it cannot serve (free rank-0 members silently hijack the happy path if mis-placed).
  Single-user ToS ONLY (free tiers are personal-account; legitimate for a solo gateway, revisit if it ever
  fronts anyone but the operator) — see [charon-free-tier-routing], [charon-pools-redesign]. Relates the
  pools-redesign capability engine (#14) and the P3 resource-availability work. Design/spec first, then
  implement. Suggested agent: strong tier for spec; frontier for the gateway-sort/failover integration
  (the spill's interaction with cost_class ordering + cooldown is design-sensitive).
  SMART SWITCH-TIMING (operator, 2026-07-08 — a REQUIREMENT, not just spill-on-error): the spill must be
  smart about WHEN to switch, not merely reactive to a 402. Net rule: switch at the LEAST-DISRUPTIVE
  BOUNDARY that still avoids running dry, driven by usage-remaining + task/session boundaries.
  (a) PREFER TASK BOUNDARIES — not mid-task (mid-task swaps carry context but can shift style even between
  equivalents). (b) PREDICT exhaustion from METER-MODEL-PROVIDER's usage/quota-remaining signal — do NOT
  wait for the hard 402. (c) RIDE IT OUT: if a low leg has enough headroom to finish the current task (or
  session), keep using it and switch at task end — or even session end if usage is low-but-sufficient;
  don't switch early just because a leg is getting low. (d) NEVER run dry mid-task: if predicted usage
  won't finish the task, switch at task START / last safe boundary instead. The forecast comes from
  METER-MODEL-PROVIDER (the sensor); the leg-swap is the PFF-P2 cross-model substitution (this ticket is
  the actuator). See POOLS-REDESIGN-ADR-v2.md "quality-equivalent, drain-ordered tier redundancy" for the
  matching tier-redundancy requirement this timing rule serves.
  D&S: depends_on COST-RANK-AUTO (cost_class enum must exist first); sequence AFTER the failover/cost
  cluster (PROXY-FAILOVER-FIX, DRAIN-ROUTING, COST-RANK-AUTO) since it shares gateway.py; concurrency —
  shares gateway.py with that cluster, so NOT parallel-safe with them; disjoint from the bridge/bench work.
note: PARKED — filed from the institutionalization audit (no prior ticket for free-tier quota tracking/spill).
  Staged so no droid claims ahead of manager gating; un-park + author the prompt at activation.
